  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84002,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84002.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 4,
    "turn_text": "I totally agree. The midterm elections was really interesting to me. A couple of things. One, I understand the value of political consultants. I'm not trying to downgrade political consultants, but they don't know all and they don't see all. The number one thing that we really weren't seeing was all of our polling packages were saying, the big four issues were crime, immigration, the economy, the border, stuff's going on in schools, the gender transitioning stuff, which has been disastrous for students, closing schools that Democrats did. And I'm not discounting any of that. I think those are all major issues in America still are actually. But one of the key issues that's really hurt us is how the left has weaponized college students. And I'm really focusing on college age voters with respect to abortion. And it's something where as a party, we didn't have a message, we didn't have a strategy. It's something that we just looked at polling and said, oh, well, that's issue number seven. But yeah, for most voters in the midterm election, it is issue number seven. But the Democrats found a way to weaponize college students with it. and they turned them out in large numbers. And that's in part one of them. That's actually the biggest reason the midterm elections didn't turn out the way we had anticipated.",
